Subject: EYES: Bates exercises (was Keratotomy)

> >The idea of Bates is that
> >poor vision comes about through certain muscles and neuro-kinetic
> >patterns becoming to rigid, and that by learning new vision habits,
> >the visual system becomes more fluid and relaxed.
> 
> By what mechanism, exactly, does the Bates method change the shape of the
> eyeball?  Since the eye obeys the laws of optics, changing the shape of the
> eyeball is the only way to overcome myopic/hyperopic disorders without some
> external correction.  How does the Bates method deal with astigmatism
> (deviation of the surface of the lens from approximating a sector of a
> sphere)?

Well, I know in my case this (Bates) sounds reasonable. There is nothing
necessarily wrong with my eyes save muscle problems. I was told by a couple
of opthamologists that it is common for young people to ruin their eyes via
a number of activities, but that it is generally a matter of conditioning,
or lack thereof. They have often suggested various excercises to improve my
vision (and I have never heard of this Bates method - sounds interesting).

In my case, I did a lot of reading books under the covers via flashlight,
and sitting 12" in front of a computer screen in dark rooms for weeks at
a time. And, to a point, I still have some of these bad habits. To boot,
I am one of many to whom fluorescent lighting is a curse. The more I am 
continuously exposed to it, the worse my vision becomes. But, the point is,
I've been told that it is certainly possible that by changing my vision
habits, doing certain excercises, and so forth, I can probably improve if
not completely correct my vision. As it happens, I work full time under
fluorescent lighting staring at a 19" monitor all day, so I think I would
be fighting an uphill battle.

So what I am saying is there are a number of us whose vision has deteriorated,
not by physical ailment, but by environment, who can probably benefit from
things like this Bates method, whatever it is. Certainly the 5-year old with
the coke-bottle glasses doesn't fall into this category ... :-)

