Date: Mon, 23 Aug 93 15:14:54 PDT
From: brt!robert@uu.psi.com (Robert Blumen)
Subject: EYES: Bates exercises (was Keratotomy)

	Ahem.  I think the Bates exercises are held in pretty low esteem by most
	ophthalmologists; I recall Martin Gardner devoting an entire chapter to
	them in his big "debunking" book (the title is something like _Fads and
	Fallacies_?).  Not that I'm a huge fan of Martin Gardner (as R.A.W. says,
	"I wish I could be as sure of anything as Martin Gardner is of
	everything."), but the Bates regime sounds pretty far-fetched to me, too.

	>The idea of Bates is that
	>poor vision comes about through certain muscles and neuro-kinetic
	>patterns becoming to rigid, and that by learning new vision habits,
	>the visual system becomes more fluid and relaxed.

	By what mechanism, exactly, does the Bates method change the shape of the
	eyeball?  Since the eye obeys the laws of optics, changing the shape of the
	eyeball is the only way to overcome myopic/hyperopic disorders without some
	external correction.  How does the Bates method deal with astigmatism
	(deviation of the surface of the lens from approximating a sector of a
	sphere)?

My understanding (though I can't remember this too clearly) is that 
the Bates people have a non-standard model of how focussing works.  
They claim that the experiments which established the standard model
were not conclusive.  What I recall is that they believe that the shape
of the eyeball is influenced by the tension in certain muscles that 
according to standard anatomy and physiology are thought to have no
function.  By retraining these muscles through chainging ones visual
habits, the shape of the eyeball can change.  They also think that 
the elasticity of the lens is not a problem, since focussing does not
occur by the lens changing shape, but rather by the eyeball changing
shape.

I don't know whether there have been any controlled studies done. 
Like many of these things, there is a certain amount of anecdotal
evidence.  I can look in some of the books I have and see if they
mention any studies.  I seem to recall some studies of some sort,
but I can't remember beyond this.

Robert

