>Other schemes have been proposed, such as a railgun up a mountainside.  It
>seems obvious that such are a big win, given the huge amount of fuel which
>is expended in the first few thousand feet.  Why haven't they been tried?
>
>Also, how significant are proximity to the equator and elevation above
>sea level?  And what about scramjets to save on oxidizer weight?

Some random thoughts:

* within limits, gaining elevation isn't a big deal.  "The huge amount of
fuel ... expended in the first few thousand feet" is mostly used to gain
velocity.

* the equator isn't the ideal launch site, because the atmosphere is
thicker there, offsetting the gain in velocity with respect to the center
of the earth.

* Using scramjets would increase development cost

* for more information on tethers see, "The Hypersonic Skyhook" in last
month's _Analog_ (cover story).  That design had about the same payback as
Dani's system (3 flights), but a lot fewer moving parts (locomotives et
al).

* Dani, why have a "landing platform" at the base of the tether?  Do you
mean that the tether must support the mass of the shuttle?  At least in the
early stages, it would be better to hover and drop off the payload (as
shown on the cover of _Analog_).

