Date: Tue, 24 Aug 93 0:05:03 PDT
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: SPACE: DC-X Flies

Ray Cromwell:
> p.s. Besides the asteroids and comets, is there any evidence that
> Jupiter/Saturn's moons have any volatiles on them? 

Europa, Ganymede, and Callisto all have surface ice and dark
sooty areas that probably contain complex organics, similar
to those found on comets.  The interaction of solar wind,
solar UV, cosmic rays and the icy and tarry surfaces of these
bodies makes for quite interesting chemistry, and alas rather 
speculative when we get into the complex hydrocarbons, life precursors 
(eg amino acids), etc.  Io looks like it's depleted of most water
and organics, but it is the most differentiated body
in the solar system and its sulfur-rich chemistry will likely be 
quite fascinating once we figure out what the heck is going on there.
Jupiter has captured several asteroids that probably contain
organic material, water and ammonia of hydration or even ice, depending 
on their origin.  

Saturn's moon Titan probably has a complex
chemistry involving hydrocarbons, polymers, etc. and methane
or ethane oceans, but it's pretty speculative at this point what is 
really going on there.  Cassinni might make it out there some
day.

Jupiter's and Saturn's moons, Jupiter Trojans, and outer solar 
system comets (Charon, Kuiper Belt, etc. but closer than the Oort 
comets) provide a huge source of volatiles over the long term,
but over the near term the Jupiter-family comets, orbiting
between Jupiter and the inner solar system, provide a more
convenient source that can be easily diverted into orbits around 
Earth and Mars and throughout the asteroid belt.  There might
be closer sources of ice, eg comets that evolved into Apollo-Amor
objects, but that's still speculative.

> ...exactly how we can extract
> and perform industrial processes on asteroids

Nobody's done anything "exact" in this area.
I've been concentrating on Jupiter-family comet volatiles lately, 
cf. my recent post, because of the general lack of work in that 
promising and necessary area, and also the low up-front costs
for the processing equipment compared to asteroid mining make it
more attractive in the near term.  Of course the two are 
complementary in terms of materials extracted; you can't get
steel from an comet, and your probably can't cheaply get water
out of a nickel-iron asteroid.  There's a good literature out 
there on asteroid mining.  In the works, to be published Real Soon 
Now is a big, thick tome by John Lewis that should cover in
detail what he talked about in general in _Space
Resources: Breaking the Bonds of Earth_, which is the
best general introduction to the use of materials native 
to space. 

On the space-tech mailing list (space-tech@cs.cmu.edu)
several of us net.folks are discuss comet mining, Big Dumb 
Boosters, and some other stuff with a fair amount of technical
detail.

>Doesn't microgravity
>make it hard to transfer traditional processes to space?

Yes, but given "skyhook" tether capabilities, it's
relatively easy to spin up million-ton industrial facilities
to 1G.  The "paydirt processes" will be those processes
that benefit from microgravity (alloying immiscible materials,
electrophoresis, etc.), but many processes will work better
in 1G.  In most cases, processes can be theoretically redesigned
for microgravity, but in practice we will likely tend
to be lazy and conservative and go with spinning up tethers
and operating in the 1G region.  With good computer simulation it
shouldn't take too long to identify the paydirt processes that
benefit most for micrograv, vac, and plasma processing.  (The
work done on combining such processes with native materials
is near zero since the O'Neill people touched on it in the
1970s, which demonstrates the huge gulf between the several
cultures with interests in space).

