From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: EYES: laser keratotomy

thamilto@pcocd2.intel.com (Tony Hamilton - FES ERG~) writes:

>Someone was telling me that a technique for using lasers to effectively
>"shave" the lens of your eye, shaping it correctly, is being developed and
>will soon be available as a much better method of correcting your vision.

Here in the UK, it is already available -- albeit as a pilot project.
Cost is c. 400 UK pounds per eye (cheaper than keratotomy). A
UV-wavelength laser is used to dig a thin circular (or elipsoidal)
groove out of the surface of the cornea. This causes the cornea to
bulge slightly, acting as a secondary lens.

Drawbacks: the technique is new, there's some pain for up to 36 hours
after the operation, and nobody knows how long it lasts yet.

Advantages: it is much less damaging than keratotomy (thin circular groove in
corneal surface v.s. penetrating radial knife-wounds), faster (c. 10
minutes under local anaesthetic), cheaper (no need for full surgical
team), and apparently shorter wavelength UV lasers are being tested
that make it less painful.

A detailed article in New Scientist discussed this treatment in the
past six months. The point was made that laser surgery is less
advanced in the US than in Europe because of the cost of medical
insurance against mishaps involving leading-edge surgical techniques.

I plan to wait a year or two, then have my eyes done if it's feasible
-- one at a time. (It may not be practical, though. I have a _very_
strong blink reflex, plus complications relating to retinal scarring.)


