From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: SPACE: DC-X Flies

Robert Brooks <rb@hprrb.rose.hp.com> writes:

>Dani proposes using locomotives to give a launch vehicle a running start,
>certainly a reasonable approach.

>Other schemes have been proposed, such as a railgun up a mountainside.  It
>seems obvious that such are a big win, given the huge amount of fuel which
>is expended in the first few thousand feet.  Why haven't they been tried?

Several reasons. 

First, the locomotives. Has Dani factored in the weight of the cable?
I'd have thought that several kilometres -- or tens of kilometres --
of cable with enough tensile strength to drag a 15,000 lb spacecraft
along at >1G is going to have a non-zero weight.

Second, the SSTO concept is marginal. Only advanced materials
technology makes it possible; see the interminable discussions 
in the sci.space archives. There's nothing special about the specific
impulse of the DC/X RL-10's relative to, say, the shuttle; it's the
ability to use a lightweight airframe and several (redundant)
lightweight engines that makes DC potentially useable. 

Third, you're adding a single point of failure with possible
abort-mode problems if you go for the cable-boost idea.  Think:
the cable attaches to the rocket somewhere.  What if the latch
fails at low speed, early during a launch?  You've got a fuelled
up rocket, without enough thrust to get up to speed on its own,
at low altitude, over land.  Yes, it's got to be over land --
otherwise there's nothing to put the railway tracks on.  Delta
Clipper, in contrast, has built-in redundancy on a massive scale; the
write-ups I've seen suggest that it can survive three simultaneous
engine failures (!) at most points in the mission profile, and still
land safely. While the cable-boost idea, if a failure occurs during
take-off, probably digs a very big hole in the ground.

>Also, how significant are proximity to the equator and elevation above
>sea level?  And what about scramjets to save on oxidizer weight?

Scramjets?

An Australian university department claims to have gotten a test-bed
scramjet to work after thirty years of trying. There are rumours that
one of the USAF "Aurora" projects uses a hypersonic pulse-detonation
engine (even rumours that the USAF is operating a TSTO winged orbiter
for putting small spy-sats into orbit for tactical reconnaisance). But
I wouldn't call hypersonic air-breathers a mature technology -- and
what we're talking here is a cheap orbiter, not a leading-edge
research project.

>My intuition says that combining all of these ideas could dramatically
>reduce launch expense.  Dani, care to peg how much?

Seconded. How many of Dani's ideas have been sufficiently researched
that they can be worked, off-the-shelf? Have any ever been tested?
Flown? Costed?

It's a nice thought, and the orbital tether is a bloody good idea that
will probably come about sooner or later, but I'm not at all sure you
could do it on a shoestring with today's off-the-shelf kit.


>Robert

